<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tionontati, Tobacco Nation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Petun, or Tionontati, were an Iroquoian people living south of Georgian Bay in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, neighbours and close allies of the Wendat. The French named them after the tobacco they grew. Dispersed by Iroquois attacks in 1649-1650, they merged with Wendat refugees to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petun.jpg" alt="Petun" width="320" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noahedits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petun_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Petun emerged in the Late Ceramic period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> archaeology, which began around 1,100 years ago, and belong with the Wendat and Neutral peoples to the Ontario Iroquois tradition. In that period farming grew in importance, settlements of longhouses were often enclosed by palisades, and burial customs came to include secondary burials and ossuaries. Shortly before contact with Europeans the northern groups had formed a Huron-Petun cultural grouping, distinct from the Neutrals and the Erie to the south.</p><p>Their name, Tionontati, is rendered as the people of the place where the mountain stands. The French name comes from pétun, a word of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupi">Tupi</a> origin for tobacco. Champlain first used it for one village he visited in 1616, but no contemporary French account mentions tobacco growing among the Petun, and the claim first appears in notes to a map of 1632.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Like the Wendat, the Petun formed a tribal confederacy, made up of two groups the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> called the Nation of the Wolves and the Nation of the Deer. Champlain counted about eight villages in 1616 and the Jesuits nine in 1639. One modern estimate puts their number at about 8,000, falling to about 3,000 by 1640 after epidemics. The Jesuit missionaries noted that the Wendat and Petun were close allies who had once fought bitter wars with each other. The Petun were also allied with the Neutrals and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a>, and in 1652 the Jesuit Relations described tattooing as almost universal among them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The French traded chiefly with the Wendat, which kept the Petun from direct access to European goods. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> the Haudenosaunee, supplied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> firearms, destroyed the Wendat villages in 1648 and 1649, and Wendat refugees fled to the Petun. By the end of 1649 the Haudenosaunee had turned on the Petun as well. Many survivors were absorbed into Haudenosaunee society, while a large group of Petun and Wendat took refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>. Moving by stages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> area and Lake Erie, they became one people known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a>. The federally recognised Wyandotte Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> regards itself as descended chiefly from the Petun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muskets from the fur trade fed the Beaver Wars from 1609, in which the League fought the French and their Wendat allies and defeated and assimilated most of the Wendat (1649), <b>Petun</b> (1650), Neutral Nation (1651), Erie (1657) and Susquehannock (1680).</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>…moved whole peoples into the League: during the Beaver Wars the Haudenosaunee defeated and assimilated most of the Wendat (1649), <b>Petun</b> (1650), the Neutral Nation (1651), the Erie (1657) and the Susquehannock (1680), and in 1722 the Tuscarora, sponsored by the Oneida, were accepted as a sixth nation.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>Survivors fled; some settled near Quebec, where Wendake survives, and others joined the <b>Petun</b> to become the Wyandot.</blockquote><a href="/state/huron-confederacy">Huron Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
